# E37 -- What does seasonal-wildlife cost in FPS, and does that cost grow with the load the fort is already under? Every experiment so far has measured what the tool DOES; none has measured what it charges. DF's own achieved frame rate is readable at `enabler.calculated_fps` and the harness already samples it as `fps_achieved` at every clock probe, so the measure is DF's own number rather than wall-clock timing that would include RPC overhead. The cap is raised to 10,000 in every arm so the cap is never the binding constraint (NEVER zero — that freezes DF permanently; the rig refuses anything under 10). Two factors crossed: the tool fully on (roster on all three layers, groups + coupling + cohesion + ecology + water job + scheduler) against wholly off, under two load levels — the fort as it stands, and the same fort with sixty extra land animals placed at t0 so the unit loop carries roughly three times the work. Placement uses the tool's own headless route in BOTH arms, so the crowd is identical and only the jobs differ. A season, two replicates per arm.
run 20260918-232733 fort CTRL backup CTRL.preverify
DF v0.53.16 win64 STEAM DFHack 53.16-r1.1 world The Legendary Dimension site 32 tiles 29..29,20..20 seed WILDERPOP1
commits: DwarfCron fe08b81 seasonal-wildlife b4dd734 fps cap 10000

## Predictions (written before the run)
- the cost is negligible: fps_achieved differs by a few per cent or less between on and off at both load levels, and the tool is free for practical purposes
- a constant cost: on runs a fixed amount below off at both load levels — the jobs cost what they cost regardless of how many units exist
- a cost that scales with units: the on/off gap is wider in the crowded arms than the baseline ones, because the jobs walk units.active and the walk grows with the map
- the tool pays for itself: on runs FASTER than off in the crowded arms, because the roster and the ceiling hold the population down where the control lets it grow

